Use bloom, controlled glow, emissive materials, shadows and subtle reflections.

Avoid childish cartoon graphics, flat vector graphics and excessive interface clutter.

The game should look like a proper futuristic arcade game.

4. THE ENORMOUS OPEN ARENA

The map is one enormous, continuous, open digital arena.

This is one of the most important requirements.

The arena must be large enough for five players to ride around for an extended period, create extensive trail patterns, chase each other and develop complex survival strategies.

Arena specifications:

- One enormous playable area.
- A continuous flat digital grid surface.
- No separate rooms.
- No disconnected zones.
- No multiple maps.
- No internal obstacles.
- No buildings.
- No pillars.
- No structures.
- No internal walls.
- No barriers.
- No objects blocking the riding area.

The only physical boundaries are the outer boundaries of the arena.

The arena must have enough playable area to make five-player matches interesting rather than cramped.

Outer boundary walls

Create a clearly visible, tall, glowing futuristic energy wall around the entire perimeter.

- Bright neon appearance.
- Visible from far away.
- Proper 3D height and depth.
- Continuous perimeter with no accidental gaps.
- Clearly distinguishable from the playable surface.

Touching or crossing the energy boundary eliminates the player.

The boundary should look like an enormous glowing digital barrier.

Camera and world scale

The arena should feel enormous.

Use appropriate camera perspective and world rendering so players can understand their position and see other bikes and distant light trails.

Do not shrink the map simply because the mobile screen is small.

The camera should follow the player's bike smoothly and support the large map without exposing rendering problems.

5. MOTORCYCLE MOVEMENT — CLASSIC TRON RULES

Use classic automatic light-cycle movement.

Every bike must:

- Move forward automatically.
- Maintain continuous movement.
- Never require an acceleration button.
- Never require a brake button.
- Never require a throttle control.
- Never allow reversing.
- Never allow manually stopping the bike.

The only steering actions are:

- Turn left.
- Turn right.

Steering implementation

Use mobile swipe gestures instead of large steering buttons.

- Swipe left to turn left.
- Swipe right to turn right.
- Use clear, responsive swipe recognition.
- Turning should be smooth and visually polished.
- The bike should lean into turns.
- The motorcycle should not teleport.
- Avoid jitter, delayed turns and accidental double turns.
- Use consistent turning angles and movement rules for every player.
- Use a reliable turning cooldown or input validation to prevent impossible instant direction reversals.

The controls must feel natural and responsive.

There must be no large left/right buttons covering the game view.

The eye icon for camera switching must remain independently usable and must not accidentally trigger steering.

6. LIGHT TRAIL SYSTEM

Every living bike continuously creates a glowing vertical light wall behind it.

Each trail must:

- Follow the bike accurately.
- Have visible thickness and height.
- Have a strong neon glow.
- Match the assigned colour of its motorcycle.
- Remain in the world after the bike passes.
- Be visible to every player.
- Be synchronized across all devices.
- Form continuous, properly connected trails without gaps.

Trails must have proper collision detection.

A player is eliminated if their motorcycle collides with:

1. Their own active light trail.
2. Another living player's active light trail.
3. The outer arena boundary.

Do not allow bikes to pass through trails.

Do not allow collision detection to fail because of high movement speed or network latency.

Use reliable swept collision detection or an equivalent method to prevent fast-moving bikes from skipping through trails.

7. FIVE DIFFERENT RANDOM BIKE COLOURS

Every match must assign five different neon colours to the five players randomly.

Rules:

