11. ELIMINATION SYSTEM

Elimination is permanent for the current match.

A player is eliminated when:

- Their bike hits their own active light trail.
- Their bike hits another player's active light trail.
- Their bike hits or crosses the outer energy boundary.

When a player dies:

1. Stop their bike.
2. Play an appropriate crash animation.
3. Show a proper visual elimination effect.
4. Remove their motorcycle from the active arena.
5. Immediately remove their entire light trail/wall from the arena.
6. Remove their trail's collision objects as well.
7. Mark them as eliminated.
8. Switch their client to spectator mode.

IMPORTANT: An eliminated player's entire trail must disappear, not just the motorcycle.

The trail must no longer block or eliminate surviving players.

Elimination must be synchronized for everyone.

Do not allow eliminated players to rejoin the active match.

12. SIMULTANEOUS ELIMINATIONS

If two players hit each other's light trails at almost exactly the same time, both players must be eliminated.

This is a deliberate game rule.

Do not arbitrarily save one player because of network timing.

Use authoritative collision resolution and a small, consistent simultaneous-collision tolerance window.

If the collision events qualify as simultaneous:

- Eliminate both players.
- Remove both bikes.
- Remove both complete light trails.
- Move both players into spectator mode.
- Update the remaining-player count.
- Display the special simultaneous death message.

The exact announcement must be:

THEY KISSED THEIR DEATH

Display the names of both eliminated players beneath the announcement.

All clients must receive the same result.

13. LIVE DEATH ANNOUNCEMENTS

Whenever a player is eliminated, show a notification at the top of the gameplay screen.

The message must identify the eliminated player and the reason.

Examples:

PLAYER NAME was eliminated by PLAYER NAME

PLAYER NAME crashed into the energy boundary

For simultaneous deaths:

THEY KISSED THEIR DEATH

Show both players' names.

Requirements:

- Announcements appear for all surviving players and spectators.
- Display the correct player names.
- Identify the responsible player when a trail collision causes elimination.
- Identify the energy boundary when that causes elimination.
- Use an appropriate visual effect.
- Keep the message visible for a few seconds.
- Automatically disappear.
- Never block too much of the gameplay view.
- Synchronize the announcement across all devices.

Do not display incorrect or invented elimination reasons.

14. SPECTATOR MODE

An eliminated player must remain connected to the match as a spectator.

They must not be removed from the multiplayer room.

Spectator requirements:

- Automatically switch to spectator mode after elimination.
- Allow a free camera.
- Let spectators move the camera around the entire arena.
- Allow them to look at surviving players.
- Allow them to observe the light trails.
- Allow them to explore different camera angles.
- Keep them updated with live match events.
- Display player names and remaining-player information.
- Show elimination announcements.
- Keep spectators connected until the match ends.

Spectators must not:

- Control any surviving bike.
- Create new light trails.
- Change the score or game state.
- Interfere with active players.
- Affect collision detection.

Do not automatically force spectators to follow one player. They have chosen free-camera spectating.

15. LAST-PLAYER-STANDING WIN CONDITION

The game is a survival match.

- No time limit.
- No round timer.
- No artificial match ending.
- No automatic elimination after a certain duration.
- Continue until one player remains.

When only one living player remains:

- End the match.
- Declare that player the winner.
- Stop active gameplay.
- Prevent further steering.
- Display the winner.
- Begin the cinematic victory sequence.

All clients must agree on the final result.

If every player is eliminated in the same simultaneous event, handle it as a draw rather than leaving the match stuck.

16. CINEMATIC WINNER ANIMATION

The victory sequence is an important feature.

When only one player remains, show a cinematic celebration.

Sequence:

1. End the active match.
2. Freeze normal gameplay.
3. Transition the camera smoothly to a cinematic third-person view.
4. Focus on the winning motorcycle.
5. Show the winning bike performing a proper wheelie stunt.
6. Lift the front wheel with believable motorcycle movement.
7. Animate the bike smoothly without clipping or unnatural movement.
8. Show the neon trail glowing dramatically.
9. Display the winner's username.
10. Display the announcement:

LAST SURVIVOR — WINNER

The wheelie is a victory animation, not an additional gameplay ability.

After the stunt, transition to the final results screen.

